{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And the angel said, etc., Go with the men.</b> One needs to know why he specified saying ״with the men.״ And our Sages of blessed memory noted this and said (Numbers Rabbah 20:15): Your portion is with them, and your end is to perish from the world. And this is the way of derash, but one needs to know the meaning of the matter according to its peshat. One further needs to know why he did not say to him ״Return,״ which would have been more fitting. However, the intention of the matter is that he gave him a reason why he consents on His authority for him to go — it is for the sake of the men who came after him to go with them, for if He does not consent on His authority for him to go, they will think thoughts, for HaShem is concerned lest Balaam do something contrary to what HaShem desires to do, and there would be a desecration of the Name in the matter, heaven forbid. And according to what I explained on the verse ״for he is going״ — that he was going alone — it is possible that the angel hinted to him to go with the men and not by himself, and the intention was to nullify from him the preparation of evil that he was planning to do with his sorceries. And so he did, as it is written: ״וַיֵּלֶךְ בִּלְעָם עִם שָׂרֵי בָלָק״." "<b>But only the word, etc., that you shall speak, etc.</b> Here He stipulated upon him a different condition from what He told him in the first permission, namely that in the first permission He forbade him the act, as it is written (Numbers 22:20): ״אַךְ אֶת הַדָּבָר וגו׳ אֹתוֹ תַעֲשֶׂה״, and from this the wicked one devised thoughts to do evil, for his power lay both in act and in speech — in act, through his sorceries and divinations, and also through the evil eye, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Zohar vol. 3, 187b) on the verse ״וַיִּשָּׂא עֵינָיו וַיַּרְא אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc., that he wished to gaze upon them with the evil eye until the Holy One, blessed be He, came and covered them in His shadow, as it is written (Numbers 24:2): ״וַתְּהִי עָלָיו רוּחַ ה׳״, and our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Zohar vol. 1, 201b): ״upon him״ — upon Israel, that his evil eye should not rule over them; and in speech — to curse them. And the intention of the Most High in the utterance ״אֹתוֹ תַעֲשֶׂה״ was meant to forbid him also the speech that would issue from him as an act, but he did not understand it, on account of his evil disposition. Therefore the word of HaShem came a second time and closed his mouth, and said to him explicitly regarding speech, as it is written: ״אֹתוֹ תְדַבֵּר״, and in this was included the prohibition of evil counsel against Israel — which the wicked one did not heed in any detail of the warning. And see the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:18) on the verse ״וַיָּשֶׂם ה׳ דָּבָר בְּפִי בִלְעָם״, that they expounded that He placed a shield of iron in his mouth: when he wished to speak evil, it would choke him. We also find that he gave evil counsel through which twenty-four thousand of Israel fell, and also in the detail of the act, which he inclined toward — not to gaze upon them with his evil eye — we find that he transgressed this as mentioned. And were it not for HaShem who was for us. And from now on I have found a reason for Israel's act in killing him, for according to the law he was liable to the death penalty, for HaShem had warned him — both regarding the act and regarding speech — explicitly as mentioned, and the warning of the children of Noah is punishable by death (Sanhedrin 57a)."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:03.214884075Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}